sequenceDiagram
  %% status: reviewed
  %% command: proof:run
  %% kind: proof.run
  autonumber
  participant CLI as CI job / CLI
  participant GW as Gateway
  participant GH as GitHub
  participant Q as CI queue<br/>FIFO
  participant SW as Swamp
  participant VM as Proof VMs
  CLI->>GW: Record the proof
  %%> Record commit, authenticated caller and options, then queue admission. Only an active authenticated ci-runner Node uses the CI pool. Operator proofs join task admission.
  GW-->>CLI: Follow one proof Operation
  GW->>GH: Select commands<br/>check trust
  %%> Read the trusted commit, selection list and cases. A fork needs approval of this head and run-proofs. No selected command succeeds without a VM.
  GW->>GH: Read cases at<br/>merge base and head
  %%> Read the selected command files and batch definitions at both commits. A command code change selects its whole cases file. Empty or malformed selected files fail.
  GW->>GW: Compare case<br/>definitions by id
  %%> For cases-only changes, select added and changed entries and every variant of each. Deleted entries select nothing. Ignore YAML formatting and comments.
  GW->>GW: Include users of<br/>changed dependencies
  %%> Follow fixture uses and file vars, including nested uses. A changed batch setup selects its users; changed fleet settings select all users. Keep the selected cases in their batches with the required setup.
  GW->>GW: Record case ids<br/>and reasons
  %%> Gateway and CI planning use the same rules and shared test fixtures. Report changed, added, uses changed fixture, uses changed var, batch setup changed or command code changed for each selected case. Zero selected cases succeeds without a VM.
  GW->>GW: Plan selected cases<br/>in batches
  %%> Expand selected variant ids only. Sum measured case seconds from prior reports; unseen cases use the measured median, or one second with no history.
  GW->>Q: Join by proof Operation id
  %%> Earlier open CI proofs without admitted runs block later proofs, including while selection is being read. Cancellation, failure and no selection remove a proof from admission.
  Q->>GW: Admit the front proof
  %%> Under the admission lock, take every currently free usable CI place, at least one and at most the batch count. A full pool or stale base waits, up to two hours, then fails clearly. Never refuse capacity or skip green.
  GW->>GW: Balance whole batches<br/>by measured time
  %%> Longest measured batch first; assign each to the least loaded VM. Selected cases of a batch stay together. Record expected cases and all shard operations atomically.
  GW->>SW: Start one run per VM
  SW->>VM: Claim a slot
  %%> Fresh VM from the host base at the trusted commit. No CI keep-failed option.
  SW->>VM: Read the workspace commit
  SW->>SW: Check the workspace commit
  loop each assigned batch, in order
    SW->>VM: Restore the head checkout
    %%> A prior new-case proof may have switched to the merge base. Restore the admitted head before planning the next batch.
    SW->>VM: Read the command batches
    SW->>SW: Select the assigned batch
    SW->>VM: Restore the base snapshot
    SW->>VM: Run selected cases<br/>with batch setup
    SW->>VM: Prove new cases fail
    VM-->>SW: Verdict lines
  end
  SW->>VM: Release the slot
  %%> Delete after every outcome. Failed or missing cleanup schedules normal group cancellation. A live or releasing VM holds its place until confirmed deletion.
  SW-->>GW: Report verdicts<br/>full artifact
  %%> Capture the full report, not its 4 KiB history tail. Identify command and file on each line. Missing, duplicate or unexpected case verdicts fail the shard; a missing expected case is unrun.
  GW->>GW: Merge all shard verdicts
  %%> Wait for every admitted child, require every expected case and every selected command, and close one proof result. Failed, cancelled, unrun and missing verdicts fail.
  GW-->>CLI: One merged report
  CLI->>CLI: Require the proof verdict
  %%> The proof job is in Required checks. A selected command with a failed case or no verdict fails. No selected command passes. Capacity is only a bounded wait, never a successful no-case result.
  %%> A fork uses the outside proof's status on this exact head. A missing, pending or failed status fails Required checks; rerun the failed Required job after the approved outside proof succeeds.
